---
description: Drive the graphic novel through the brahma MCP tools
alwaysApply: true
---

This project is a Cursor harness. The person tells a story and does not run commands.

Use the brahma MCP tools: novel_plan, novel_settings, novel_refs, novel_character, novel_draw, novel_sequence, novel_edit, novel_undo, novel_review, novel_assemble, novel_health.

novel_plan starts a story and creates a new folder in workspace, with settings.json for the look (art, palette, line, rendering, lighting, negative, notes). Pass setup when the person names an art type or a palette. Continue an existing story with its project path; do not call novel_plan again for the same book.

When the person attaches a picture, pass its path. role style is a graphic reference. role sketch is a hand drawing: look at the file and fill reading with art, palette, line, rendering, lighting, and notes from what you see, in English. If the drawing shows one character, set id to that character so it travels only with that character and never to places or the style sample. If the tool returns needs_reading, look at the pending file and call novel_settings with that setup. role character or location is for one person or place; set id.

When a tool returns jobs, generate one image per job, in parallel when the jobs do not depend on each other. Copy `description` verbatim into the image tool, set `aspect_ratio`, and pass `reference_image_paths` unchanged. Then hand the file back to the tool that asked: novel_refs or novel_draw with `images: [{key, path, accept, note}]`, novel_sequence op place, or novel_edit with image. The tool writes `dest` inside the story folder and keeps every file in takes/. A picture you reject goes back with accept false and a note, so the person can still open it. Never give the person a path under `.cursor/projects/.../assets`.

Every panel is built in this order, written in stone: first the place, then the blocking the person approves, then the characters, then a filter only if the person wants one. The place is drawn empty (the plate: no people, no effects). The blocking says where each character stands and how tall it is, for a sequence panel and for a page panel alike: a stick figure over the plate, feet on its spot, numbered in drawing order, the dashed horizon line, and each height as a share of the frame (alto). A paper drawing is that same blocking: read each stick figure from the top of the head to the feet as fractions of the frame drawn around it. Look at the plate and set the horizon (0..1 from the top); scale_off flags a standing character too big or too small for where its feet are, so say it and propose another box. On a static camera the standing figures already accepted on that plate lock one ground ratio (ground_ratio): a new figure's height is (feet − horizon) / that ratio, and anyone more than 15% off is scale_off even when the new figures agree with each other. The first panel, and a moving camera, use the median of the panel being drawn. A page panel does not take another panel's ground. Never draw a figure before the blocking is approved: show the preview and wait for the person's yes, then novel_sequence op accept step blocking, or novel_draw approve_blocking for a page panel. A changed box, pose, horizon, or plate takes the approval back. Each character is drawn alone, full body, on flat green; the tools key it out and set it into its box with the feet on the bottom edge, back to front. Outside the figure and its shadow the place stays pixel for pixel. A sequence sets where the shadows fall with shadow {back, lean, strength} (back > 0 runs up the picture, away from the camera; lean < 0 goes to the left of the picture), so every figure in the shot throws its silhouette the same way; without it there is only a soft shadow under the feet. novel_draw and novel_sequence op frame return these steps one job at a time; do not ask for a whole panel in one image.

Effects stay out of every base image: sparks, glowing specks, fireflies, bokeh, lens flare, light rays, glow, fog, mist, haze, rain, snow, smoke, embers. The tools move them out of the look, the places, and the actions into settings.json effects, and keep them in the negative. A filter is drawn only when asked, over a finished panel (novel_draw effects, novel_sequence op filter), into a separate -fx.png that is its own layer in the page and never a reference.

No two figures share a pose. In one panel each figure has its own pose and stride; the tools refuse a blocking with two equal poses, warn with pose_repeated when a figure keeps its pose from panel n-1 unless the beat says it stands still, and novel_review returns duplicate_pose when two figures look alike. Say so and redraw only that figure.

Which picture a new image may look at. A page panel: its plate looks only at the place plate, never at the style sample (often a portrait, whose framing and particles a plate would copy); each figure looks at its character sheet, turnaround view, and pose sheet; never at another page panel. A sequence is one continuous shot: a static camera shares one plate, a moving camera draws plate n from plate n-1; each figure looks at its turnaround view, pose sheet, and the same figure in panel n-1 (its cutout on green), only after n-1 is accepted. These are the only two rules.

The pose sheet (refs/characters/<id>-poses.png) is drawn after the turnaround: face close-ups on top, full-body action poses below. Set the poses with novel_character poses (up to six, for example the actions of the coming sequence).

Anatomy and one-sided marks live in the character, not in this rule. Use novel_character to set body (the body plan, for example six limbs or four walking legs) and marks [{part, side: wearer_left|wearer_right|center, text}]. The tools translate the side for each view: a mark on the wearer's left is on the right of the picture in a front view and on the left in a back view.

To change one part of a picture, such as a hand or a lamp, use novel_edit with the region and the change. Only the region is pasted back; the rest stays pixel for pixel. Do not redraw a whole sheet to fix one part.

When the person says deshacer or rehacer, call novel_undo. It restores the previous installed take of that picture, or the most recently installed one if they do not name it. Open the restored file. Do not redraw it. Redo walks forward. A new picture after an undo drops the redo. sequence.json stays as it is; if the tool names later accepted panels, say so.

Before accepting a sheet, a turnaround, a pose sheet, or a panel, call novel_review and open the crops. Judge the crops, not the thumbnail. If the change the person asked for is not clearly visible, say so and do not accept it.

A sequence lives in panels/<name> with sequence.json: action, place, time, camera (static or moving), shot, figures [{name, character, pose}] front to back, blocking per panel [{figure, box, pose, view}] (view only when that figure turns from the shot's view, for example back when it looks behind it), horizon, beats (one action per panel), characters, view, effects, shadow, and an optional brush_box. A box may run past the bottom of the frame when a figure walks out of it feet first. After the plate is accepted, novel_sequence op frame returns the blocking with status needs_approval and writes panel-NN-blocking.png; show it to the person and wait for the yes before any figure (op stage redraws it). Draw what the beat says. Do not replace a patrol with people standing in a line.

Two sequence functions. The person can ask for them, and you suggest them when a sequence drifts. Revisar las referencias is novel_sequence op review: it compares each panel with the previous one, region by region, before any redraw; report what moved in the camera, the path, the sky, the poses, the side of each mark, and the head scale. Unificar el arte is novel_sequence op unify, front to back: the accepted previous panel carries the paint, the palette, and the finish; the panel being unified keeps its own camera and action. A panel that did not inherit the previous paint is not accepted and is not the reference for the next one.

When the person asks for a flipbook and the sequence has at least three accepted panels, call novel_sequence op flipbook. Open the mp4 it writes in the sequence folder. The frames between the panels are a motion estimate; a hard cut warps. Do not redraw the sequence for this. With fewer than three accepted panels, say how many are missing.

Pages are OpenRaster files; give the person those paths.
